This is a classic quirk of downloading files via APIs in React!
Even though you perfectly updated the Spring Boot backend to send the correct filename in the Content-Disposition header, your React frontend is likely intercepting the file as a blob and forcing its own hardcoded name when it creates the download link.
If you are using document.createElement("a") to trigger the download in your frontend (likely inside your **VoucherBulkUpload.js** file where the auto-download happens), the frontend will override the backend's name.
Here is how you fix the frontend so it generates the exact same date format (Error_Report - YYYY-MM-DD.xlsx).
### The Fix (In your React Frontend)
Open the file where the auto-download is triggered (most likely **VoucherBulkUpload.js**). Find the logic that handles the file download—it will look very similar to this:
```javascript
const blob = new Blob([response.data]);
const url = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);
const link = document.createElement("a");
link.href = url;

// YOUR CODE PROBABLY LOOKS LIKE THIS RIGHT NOW:
// link.setAttribute("download", "Error_Report.xlsx"); 

```
**Replace the setAttribute line with this:**
```javascript
// 1. Generate today's date in YYYY-MM-DD format
const currentDate = new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0];

// 2. Dynamically set the file name
link.setAttribute("download", `Error_Report - ${currentDate}.xlsx`);

```
### Why this happens:
When you download a file using an API client like axios or fetch (which your callApi hook uses), the browser doesn't automatically read the backend's filename header because of CORS security rules. Instead, it relies strictly on whatever string you pass into the link.setAttribute("download", "...") method in JavaScript.
Once you update that specific line in your upload component, the downloaded file will instantly take on the correct date!
